{"candidates":[{"claim":"A decision procedure strictly polynomial in the number of coins n alone is achievable, running in O(n^3) time, and it correctly finds the smallest counterexample for non-canonical coin systems.","counterconditions":["If the smallest counterexample is not within the restricted polynomial-sized set of candidates evaluated by Pearson's algorithm, it will falsely conclude canonicity.","If bit complexity is considered and arithmetic operations cost O(log c_1), the running time becomes O(n^3 log c_1), violating the strictly polynomial-in-n claim.","If the algorithm fails to correctly handle base cases where n < 3, it might produce incorrect results or errors."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"06be33f59f4066be","quote":"Produce a decision procedure that, given C, decides canonicity, and when C is not canonical also returns the SMALLEST counterexample amount. The procedure must"},{"block":"0d288670fc60167e"},{"block":"0c2bebf8bed87d67"}],"mechanism":"Pearson's algorithm (2005) achieves O(n^3) time complexity by evaluating canonicity using only a polynomial number of candidate amounts. It checks optimal and greedy representations for amounts constructed from combinations of coins (specifically residues of multiples of c_i modulo c_j). It finds the minimal counterexample among these candidates without depending on the magnitude c_1.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8}],"simulation_proposals":[]}